<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Yoreh Day'ah, Volume 1</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Laws of Melichah [Salting]</div>
<div class="halacha-title">1</div>

<div class="subtitle">
  Based upon the Torah-teaching "A Prayer of Chavakuk," Siman 19,
  as cited in Laws of Treifos, Halacha 1.
</div>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — Title &amp; Source]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">מְלִיחָה א — עַל-פִּי הַתּוֹרָה 'תְּפִלָּה לַחֲבַקּוּק' סִימָן יט הַנַּ"ל בְּהִלְכוֹת טְרֵפוֹת הֲלָכָה א</span>
</details>

<div class="notice">
  <strong>Note:</strong> Melichah Halacha #2 is included within Laws of Chailev v'Dam, Halacha 4.
  Melichah Halacha #3 is included within Laws of Dam, Halacha 1.
</div>

<hr class="divider"/>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════ SECTION 1 ═══════════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">§ 1</span>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — §1]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְזֶהוּ עִנְיַן מְלִיחָה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁאָמַר רַבֵּנוּ, נֵרוֹ יָאִיר, בַּבֵּאוּר בְּסִימָן כג; שֶׁהַמֶּלַח הוּא בְּחִינַת בְּרִית מֶלַח עוֹלָם, שֶׁהוּא מַמְתִּיק מְרִירוּתָא דְּעָלְמָא. וְעַל יָדוֹ נִפְלָט הַדָּמִים רָעִים שֶׁהֵם בִּבְחִינַת פְּגַם הַבְּרִית…</span>
</details>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the matter of <span class="ht">melichah</span> [salting] — as Rabbainu, may his
light shine, said in his commentary to Siman 23: that salt is of the aspect of
<span class="kab">bris melach olam</span> [an eternal covenant of salt], which sweetens the
bitterness of the world [<span class="kab">mamtikas merirusa d'alma</span>]. And through it
the evil bloods are expelled — which are of the aspect of the blemish of the
<span class="ht">bris</span> [covenant] — as mentioned above in Laws of Chailev v'Dam,
Halacha 5.
</p>
<p>
And this is the aspect of the <span class="em">rinsing with water</span>
[<span class="ht">ha-hadachah b'mayim</span>] — to render the meat fit [<span class="ht">l'hachshir
ha-basar</span>] by placing it and soaking it within the water. For the water purifies it
from all impurities — and in particular from the blemish of the <span class="ht">bris</span>
— of the aspect of a <span class="ht">mikveh</span> of water
[<span class="kab">mikveh mayim</span>]. For the <span class="ht">mikveh</span> is a very
great rectification for the blemish of the <span class="ht">bris</span>. For the
<span class="ht">mikveh</span> is the secret of being concealed within the waters
[<span class="kab">sod ha-hit'almus b'soch ha-mayim</span>], as is known — which is the
aspect of the rectification of the <span class="ht">bris</span> — of the aspect of the
seed of Yosef. For they are of the aspect of the fish in the sea, over whom the waters
cover, and the evil eye of the seventy nations has no power over them, as above.
<span class="note">(In Laws of Meat that Left One's Sight, Halacha 2.)</span> And therefore
the meat is rectified and rendered fit through the water — in order to remove from it the
impurity of the bloods, which are of the aspect of the blemish of the
<span class="ht">bris</span>. As our Sages of blessed memory said: "because the blood is
visible upon it" — which passes off and falls away through the water, which is the aspect
of the rectification of the <span class="ht">bris</span> — of the aspect of fish in the
sea, as above.
</p>
<p>
And this is the aspect of the <span class="em">first rinsing</span>
[<span class="ht">hadachah rishonah</span>] and the <span class="em">final rinsing</span>
[<span class="ht">hadachah acharonah</span>]. For the soul within the living creature that
has fallen from its level — from the level of speech [<span class="ht">medaber</span>] to
the level of the living creature [<span class="ht">chai</span>], as above — requires many
rectifications, one after another, until it ascends and is rectified completely — to
restore it to its root — of the aspect of <span class="ht">teshuvah</span> [repentance and
return]. For all the rectifications of the meat that one performs are of the aspect of the
rectifications of <span class="ht">teshuvah</span>. For one must in the same way rectify
the soul within the living creature — to restore it to its root — which is the aspect of
<span class="ht">teshuvah</span>.
</p>
<p>
And therefore, at the beginning, it requires <span class="ht">shechitah</span>
[ritual slaughter] — to subdue and break the all-encompassing evil
[<span class="ht">ra ha-kolel</span>], as above. For this is the beginning of
<span class="ht">teshuvah</span> — to turn away from the above-mentioned evil and to break
it. And afterward to proceed and remove all the forbidden fats
[<span class="ht">chalavin</span>] and all the forbidden veins that are found visibly, within
it — so that it not be a case of "immersing while holding a creeping thing in one's hand"
[<span class="ht">toveil v'sheretz b'yado</span>]. Therefore after one turns away from the
all-encompassing evil cited above — which is the very beginning and the start — then one
must remove all the evil things from within it. And this is accomplished easily after the
<span class="ht">shechitah</span>. For the essence of the labor and effort is the
<span class="ht">shechitah</span> — which is the subduing of the above-mentioned evil —
for which an expert and certified craftsman is required — along with the inspection of the
knife and the rest of the precautions of <span class="ht">shechitah</span>, as above. But
afterward, the <span class="ht">nikur</span> [removal of forbidden parts] is done with
relative ease — as they remove and extract from it all the rest of the evils found therein.
</p>
<p>
And then one merits the aspect of the <span class="ht">mikveh</span> of water. And this is
the aspect of the first rinsing. And afterward it must be left to rest in its salt, as
above. And that is the completion of the rectification — which is the final rinsing.
For <span class="verse">"one who comes to purify himself is helped"</span> — and they say
to him: "Wait" <span class="note">(as is cited in the words of Rabbainu, may his light
shine, Siman 6)</span>. For it is impossible to enter into <span class="ht">teshuvah</span>
all at once — and one must wait and tarry and endure much shedding of blood
[<span class="ht">shfikas damim</span>], in order to subdue the evil blood in the left
ventricle of the heart that was strengthened through his sins.
</p>
</div>

<hr class="divider"/>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════ SECTION 2 ═══════════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">§ 2</span>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — §2]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְזֶה בְּחִינַת הַשְּׁהִיָּה שֶׁצָּרִיךְ לִשְׁהוֹת בְּמִלְחוֹ וְלִהְיוֹת כָּבוּשׁ בְּתוֹךְ הַצִּיר בִּכְלִי מְנֻקָּב…</span>
</details>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the aspect of the <span class="em">waiting period</span>
[<span class="ht">ha-shehiyah</span>] — that one must wait in its salt and be pressed within
the brine [<span class="ht">tzir</span>] in a perforated vessel — of the aspect of the
above-mentioned "they say to him: Wait." In order to force out the evil bloods that were
absorbed into the flesh, that entered into the depths of the body. And therefore it is
impossible to rectify the meat with speed. For it is impossible to bring it into
<span class="ht">teshuvah</span> all at once — until it waits in its salt, which is the
aspect of rectification — the aspect of the covenant of salt — in order to sweeten its
bitterness and to expel all its bloods, as above.
</p>
<p>
And therefore it is placed between the two rinsings, which are of the aspect of the
<span class="ht">mikveh</span> of water, as above — in accordance with what is cited in the
name of the Baal Shem Tov of blessed memory. And it is explained above a number of times:
that at the beginning of <span class="ht">teshuvah</span>, one is raised from above to a
great level — higher than one's own level — and afterward one is brought back down from
there in order to be tested and refined and purified. For one must return and pass through
all those roads again — of the aspect of <span class="ht">teshuvas ha-mishkal</span>
[equivalent repentance], as above.
</p>
</div>

<hr class="divider"/>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════ SECTION 3 ═══════════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">§ 3</span>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — §3]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְזֶה בְּחִינַת הֲדָחָה רִאשׁוֹנָה וַהֲדָחָה אַחֲרוֹנָה שֶׁהֵם בִּבְחִינַת מִקְוֵה מַיִם כַּנַּ"ל…</span>
</details>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the aspect of the first rinsing and the final rinsing — which are of the
aspect of the <span class="ht">mikveh</span> of water, as above — which are a great ascent
and a very great rectification. For this is the essential aspect of
<span class="ht">teshuvah</span> — when one merits to enter into the aspect of the
<span class="ht">mikveh</span> of water, which is the aspect of
<span class="kab">alma d'asay</span> [the World to Come] — the aspect of
<span class="ht">teshuvah</span>, as above.
</p>
<p>
And at the beginning, one is raised from above to a great level, as above — to the aspect
of <span class="ht">teshuvah</span> — the aspect of the <span class="ht">mikveh</span> of
water, as above. And this is the aspect of the first rinsing. And even though one has
already merited the ultimate rectification — which is the aspect of the
<span class="ht">mikveh</span>, as above — afterward one is brought back down from there in
order to be refined and purified, as above — of the aspect of "they say to him: Wait," as
above. And this is the aspect of the fact that it must wait in its salt for a set period of
time — in order to be purified and refined and to expel all its evil bloods that were
absorbed within it, as above. And then afterward one merits a second time to complete the
rectification in truth. And this is the aspect of the final rinsing — the aspect of the
<span class="ht">mikveh</span> of water — for one then places it in again, once it has
merited to be purified, to expel its evil bloods, as above.
</p>
<p>
And this is the aspect of the Exodus from Egypt, which was of the aspect of
<span class="ht">teshuvah</span> — the aspect of <span class="ht">yovel</span>
[the Jubilee year], as is known. And at the beginning He raised them to far above their
level, as is known. And this is the aspect of <span class="ht">milah</span>
[circumcision] and <span class="ht">tevilah</span> [immersion] that they merited on the
eve of Pesach. For immediately after the <span class="ht">milah</span> — through which
they subdued the all-encompassing evil, which is the aspect of
<span class="ht">milah</span> — they immediately merited the aspect of the Exodus from
Egypt, which was the aspect of <span class="ht">teshuvah</span> — which was then far above
their level, as is known.
</p>
</div>

<hr class="divider"/>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════ SECTION 4 ═══════════════════════════════ -->
<span class="section-num">§ 4</span>

<details class="heb">
  <summary>[Hebrew — §4]</summary>
  <span class="heb-text">וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינַת הַמִּקְוֵה מַיִם שֶׁזָּכוּ אָז בְּעֶרֶב פֶּסַח מִיָּד…</span>
</details>

<div class="para-block">
<p>
And this is the aspect of the <span class="ht">mikveh</span> of water that they merited
immediately on the eve of Pesach. And afterward He brought them down from their level.
And this is the aspect of the Counting [<span class="ht">Sefirah</span>], as is known —
which is like a woman who purifies herself for her husband. Which is the aspect of
"Wait" — that one must wait and tarry in order to purify oneself from the blood of
<span class="ht">nidah</span> [menstrual separation], as above. And then they merited to
complete the rectification on <span class="ht">Shavuos</span>. And then they merited to
enter into the <span class="ht">mikveh</span> — the aspect of a supernal, very exalted
<span class="ht">mikveh</span> — which is the ultimate rectification.
</p>
<p>
And this is the aspect of the two rinsings — the aspect of the two
<span class="ht">mikvaos</span> [ritual baths] cited above — which are needed in order to
be rectified: namely, at the beginning of the <span class="ht">teshuvah</span> and at the
end of the <span class="ht">teshuvah</span>, as above. And in between is the waiting in
salt — which is the aspect of "Wait," as above — in order to expel the evil bloods, as above.
</p>
<p>
And as is explained in the Torah-teaching that speaks of this
<span class="src">(Siman 6, Likutay Moharan Vol. 1)</span>: that the above-mentioned aspect
of "Wait" is the aspect of <span class="ht">shfikas damim</span> [shedding of blood] that
one must endure, as above. And through this one merits the aspect of
<span class="ht">teshuvah</span> — which is the aspect of
<span class="kab">Ekyeh</span> [the Divine Name of pure Being] <span class="note">(as is
explained there)</span>. And this is the aspect of the <span class="ht">mikveh</span> of
water — which is the aspect of <span class="kab">Ekyeh</span>. For
<span class="ht">mikveh</span> in <span class="ht">gematria</span> [numerical value] equals
151 — which is the numerical value of <span class="kab">Ekyeh</span> when spelled out fully —
as is known, and as above.
</p>
<p>
And as is already explained in Laws of Pesach, Halacha 1: that therefore the measure of
salting is eighteen [<span class="ht">chai</span>] minutes — for this is the aspect of the
rectification of the <span class="ht">bris</span> — in order to return to life
[<span class="ht">chaim</span>]. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: that
the measure is [the time required to walk] one <span class="ht">mil</span> [a Talmudic
mile]. For a <span class="ht">mil</span> is two thousand cubits — the
<span class="ht">techum Shabbos</span> [the Shabbos boundary] — which is the aspect of the
boundary of holiness [<span class="ht">gevul ha-kedushah</span>], as mentioned above
<span class="note">(in Laws of Techumin, Halacha 1)</span>. And this is the very same
measure of eighteen minutes — for there is life [<span class="ht">chai</span>]
<span class="note">(as is explained there)</span>.
</p>
<p>
And therefore one must wait through the rectification of salting for this measure — in
order to merit to return within the boundary and domain of holiness
[<span class="ht">gevul u'tchum ha-kedushah</span>] — in order to merit life — the aspect
of <span class="verse">"<span class="ht">tzadik chai olaimin</span>"</span>
[the <span class="ht">tzadik</span> lives forever].
</p>
<p>
And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: roasting [<span class="ht">tzli</span>]
requires no salting. For fire is the rectification — of the aspect of:
<span class="verse">"Everything that comes into fire, you shall pass through fire, and it
will be purified"</span> <span class="src">[Numbers 31:23]</span>. And it consumes the
filth [<span class="kab">zuhama</span>]. But when one merits to attach oneself to the
<span class="ht">tzadik</span> — who is of the aspect of the covenant of salt — then one
receives from him the aspect of saltiness [<span class="ht">milcha</span>] — the aspect of
rectifications — to sweeten his bitterness. And then one is saved from the fire through
which one would have had to be refined, G-d forbid — of the aspect of
<span class="verse">"you shall pass through fire"</span>, as above. For
<span class="verse">"that which is salted is like that which is boiling"</span>
[<span class="ht">maliyach harei hu k'roteach</span>] — like roasting that is roasted in
fire. For through the aspect of salting — which is the aspect of attaching oneself to the
<span class="ht">tzadik</span>, as above — one is finely purified and saved from the fire,
and emerges pure and beautiful and complete without any blemish whatsoever. And one merits
the ultimate rectification, as above.
</p>
</div>

<hr class="divider"/>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════ TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY ════════════════════ -->
<div class="summation">
  <h3>Translator's Summary</h3>
  <p>
    Reb Noson takes the halachic process of preparing meat — first rinse, salting, final
    rinse — and reads it as a precise map of the soul's journey in
    <span style="font-style:italic;color:#f5c842">teshuvah</span>.
  </p>
  <div class="flow">
    <span class="flow-item">Shechitah</span>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <span class="flow-item">Nikur</span>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <span class="flow-item">1st Rinsing (Mikveh)</span>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <span class="flow-item">Salting — 18 min. wait</span>
    <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
    <span class="flow-item">Final Rinsing (Mikveh)</span>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:1em">
    This mirrors the spiritual arc: subdue the all-encompassing evil (shechitah) → remove
    the smaller evils within (nikur) → first elevation in teshuvah (1st rinse) → the
    "Wait" — the crucible of shfikas damim in between — → complete rectification (final
    rinse). The 18-minute measure of salting is chai — life — the time needed to cross
    back into the boundary of holiness. And the tzadik, who is the covenant of salt, saves
    one from having to pass through fire alone.
  </p>
</div>


